\section{Vector Spaces}

% -------------------------------------------------
\begin{frame}[t,shrink=12]{35. Vector Spaces}

A \textbf{vector space} is a set of objects called vectors together
with vector addition and scalar multiplication satisfying specific
axioms.

Examples include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathbb{R}^2$
    \item $\mathbb{R}^3$
    \item $\mathbb{R}^n$
    \item Polynomial spaces
    \item Spaces of matrices
    \item Function spaces
\end{itemize}

A vector space must be closed under:

\[
\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v}
\]

and

\[
c\mathbf{u}.
\]

\end{frame}

% -------------------------------------------------
\begin{frame}[t,shrink=12]{36. Important Properties of Vector Spaces}

For vectors $\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}$ and scalars $a,b$:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v}$ belongs to the space.
    \item $a\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the space.
    \item Addition is commutative.
    \item Addition is associative.
    \item A zero vector exists.
    \item Every vector has an additive inverse.
    \item Scalar multiplication is compatible with vector addition.
\end{itemize}

\begin{block}{Why Vector Spaces Matter}
Vector spaces provide a common mathematical framework for studying
vectors, matrices, transformations, data and computational models.
\end{block}

\end{frame}

% -------------------------------------------------
\begin{frame}[t,shrink=12]{37. Subspaces}

A \textbf{subspace} is a subset of a vector space that is itself a
vector space under the same operations.

A non-empty subset $W$ of $V$ is a subspace if

\[
\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}\in W
\Rightarrow
\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v}\in W
\]

and

\[
\mathbf{u}\in W,\;c\in\mathbb{R}
\Rightarrow
c\mathbf{u}\in W.
\]

Examples include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Lines through the origin
    \item Planes through the origin
    \item Column spaces
    \item Null spaces
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}

